      "question": "The sum of two numbers is 6 times their geometric mean, show that the numbers are in the ratio $(3+2\\sqrt{2}) : (3-2\\sqrt{2})$.",
      "finalAnswer": "Proof",
      "solutionSteps": [
        "Let numbers be $a, b$. Given $a+b = 6\\sqrt{ab}$.",
        "$\\frac{a+b}{2\\sqrt{ab}} = \\frac{3}{1}$.",
        "Apply Componendo and Dividendo: $\\frac{a+b+2\\sqrt{ab}}{a+b-2\\sqrt{ab}} = \\frac{3+1}{3-1}$.",
        "$\\frac{(\\sqrt{a}+\\sqrt{b})^2}{(\\sqrt{a}-\\sqrt{b})^2} = \\frac{4}{2} = 2$.",
        "$\\frac{\\sqrt{a}+\\sqrt{b}}{\\sqrt{a}-\\sqrt{b}} = \\sqrt{2}$.",
        "Again C&D: $\\frac{2\\sqrt{a}}{2\\sqrt{b}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}+1}{\\sqrt{2}-1}$.",
        "Square both sides: $\\frac{a}{b} = \\frac{2+1+2\\sqrt{2}}{2+1-2\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{3+2\\sqrt{2}}{3-2\\sqrt{2}}$."
